{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01RCd9JHm2xkP2TxSXQoJPR4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Written and committed: **`answers/01-026-why-are-decoder-only-models-dominant-even-for-non-generation.md`**\n\nThe answer covers four compounding reasons decoder-only models won:\n\n1. **Unified training objective** — next-token prediction uses every token as signal, no masking or paired examples needed, scales cleanly with Chinchilla laws\n2. **One model, every task** — classify (generate a label), summarize, embed (pool last token), QA — no zoo of specialized models\n3. **In-context learning** — prompting replaces fine-tuning for task adaptation; BERT fundamentally cannot do this\n4. **Empirical scaling evidence** — all scaling laws characterize decoder-only; nobody has a Chinchilla curve for BERT-70B\n\nThe pitfall section explicitly handles the common weak-candidate trap: saying \"bidirectional attention is better for classification\" without knowing it doesn't matter at scale. The cross-encoder reranking carve-out (ms-marco-MiniLM in RAG) is called out as BERT's remaining production niche.\n\n**Next up:** Q27 — What is positional encoding and why is it needed?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":345,"cache_read_input_tokens":56574,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":345,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"45c6720b-e4f8-4cea-bdb0-9aa2d1b5bf48","uuid":"fe282ce7-9585-495f-8fe5-a61906215ae9"}
